================================================================================
 FRIENDLY MACHINES GITHUB <-> NOSTR BRIDGE
 Current implementation contract
================================================================================

1. PURPOSE AND SCOPE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The public client at https://friendly-machines.com/git/ is a standalone
NostrGit client and Git repository browser. It reads and publishes NIP-34
events directly through Nostr relays with a browser NIP-07 signer. A user does
not need auth.friendly-machines.com to use it.

The server in this directory additionally mirrors supported collaboration
metadata between GitHub and Nostr:

  Implemented in both directions:
    - repository star/unstar               Nostr kind 7 / kind 5
    - issue roots                         Nostr kind 1621
    - issue discussion comments/replies  Nostr kind 1111 / NIP-22
    - Open, Resolved, and Closed status   kinds 1630, 1631, 1632
    - valid same-author deletion requests kind 5 / NIP-09

  Accepted from Nostr but not mapped to a GitHub issue state:
    - Draft status                        kind 1633

  Not implemented by the server bridge:
    - GitHub pull requests, review comments, or inline review comments
    - Nostr patch, PR, or PR-update roots (1617, 1618, 1619)
    - issue labels, Git tags, repository ref state, or releases
    - Git repository object or ref synchronization

The browser client supports Nostr-native patches, PRs, PR updates, repository
announcements, and repository state separately. That does not imply that the
GitHub bridge supports those types.

The client retains its signature-verified event-ID map across reloads and
merges later relay observations into it. Repository-announcement publication
uses two default relays and attempts every configured relay. One acknowledgement
is sufficient for an interactive publish to succeed; unavailable relays are
reported to the user instead of making one failed relay block publication.
The bridge independently banks an observed valid announcement.

The bridge is a metadata bridge. It never clones a Git repository and never
uses a local or remote Git checkout to decide issue/comment/status state.
GitHub webhooks and REST objects contain the GitHub metadata; signed Nostr
events contain the Nostr metadata.

1.1 COLLECTION SEMANTICS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

An array on the wire is not automatically a list in the domain model. The
implementation uses the following explicit collection algebra:

Collection                         Semantic type and identity
---------------------------------  ---------------------------------------------
Signed event tags                  Ordered wire sequence. Exact outer and inner
                                   order is part of the NIP-01 event ID and
                                   signature and is never rewritten on receipt.

relays/clone/web/maintainers       Sets of strings. Duplicate and input order
tag values, p/t mentions,          have no meaning. Sets are sorted only for
filter members, deletion targets   deterministic output, display, and resource
                                   caps. Registration requires equality with
                                   the complete announced relay set.

d/name/description/subject/a/c,    Optional scalar fields. Identical duplicate
branch-name/merge-base, r:euc,     tags collapse to one value. Distinct values
K/k, E/e root/parent relations     are ambiguous and invalidate that use; no
                                   first/last tag is selected.

Relay event pool                   Map event-ID -> verified immutable event.
                                   Relay responses and the browser's durable
                                   local ledger merge; one response never
                                   replaces the known map.

Addressable event versions         Set reduced per (kind,pubkey,d) coordinate
                                   by greatest created_at, then lowest event ID.

NIP-39 identity snapshots          Complete regular-replaceable i-tag set per
                                   author. Select greatest created_at, then
                                   lowest event ID, before deletion; versions
                                   are never unioned and deletion of the head
                                   never resurrects an older snapshot.

NIP-22 comments                    Directed parent graph keyed by event ID.
                                   E/K/P define the root and e/k/p the parent.
                                   Reference edges establish causality;
                                   timestamps order siblings for display only.

NIP-34 patch messages              Directed reply graph keyed by event ID.
                                   Marked e/reply edges establish series shape.
                                   File order is used only when the client
                                   creates a new causal patch chain.

NIP-34 status events               Set of candidates per immutable root, reduced
                                   after authorization by greatest created_at,
                                   then lowest event ID.

NIP-09 deletions                   Set of (deletion ID,target ID) relations plus
                                   durable tombstones. Target arrival order has
                                   no effect; deletion-of-deletion has no effect.

Repository announcements          Durable replacement head plus an independent
and address deletions              same-author kind-5/k=30617 cutoff. Empty
                                   relay results are not set subtraction.

NIP-34 repository state            Map fully-qualified ref-name -> commit ID,
                                   plus an optional single HEAD symbolic ref.
                                   A 30618 publication is one complete snapshot,
                                   not an ordered stream of per-ref changes.

Repository/NIP-46 relay values     Sets of alternative endpoints. NIP-46
                                   switch_relays replaces the connection set;
                                   it is not a preference list. Fan-out limits
                                   are applied after deduplication and sorting.

GitHub installations/repos/users   Maps keyed by GitHub's stable numeric IDs;
                                   active repository name is also unique.
                                   Complete API reconciliation, not webhook
                                   array or arrival order, determines membership.

event_map and identity links       One-to-one maps in both directions, enforced
                                   by unique database indexes.

Queue rows                         Durable scheduled work records. run_at then
                                   row ID is only claim order; correctness and
                                   final state may not depend on execution order.
                                   dedupe_key identifies equivalent pending work.

Repository stars                   State map keyed by stable GitHub repository
                                   ID + GitHub user ID. GitHub webhooks are
                                   wake-up signals; current API state is
                                   authoritative. Nostr stars are independently
                                   signed kind-7 events; same-author kind-5
                                   relations tombstone them.

projects.list                      A true ordered presentation list. Duplicate
                                   repository identifiers are invalid.

Git commit history / tag-object    True causal sequences. Git tree entries,
chains / newly created patch set   branches, and tags are keyed collections and
                                   are sorted only for deterministic presentation.

The decisive rule is that sorting a set creates a canonical representation,
not precedence or causality. A reducer or graph edge is required whenever one
member can affect another.

2. OPTIONAL USER AUTHENTICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The GitHub App installation is server infrastructure, not a user login.
Every bridged GitHub repository must have an active, API-reconciled GitHub App
installation. That installation supplies the always-on GitHub identity needed
when no user has linked an account.

User setup                    Standalone client       GitHub -> Nostr
----------------------------  ----------------------  -------------------------
No auth                       Direct NIP-07 posting   Bridge key + gh_user tag
GitHub App user auth only     Direct NIP-07 posting   Bridge key + gh_user tag
GitHub + Nostr linked         Direct NIP-07 posting   User's NIP-46 signer
Nostr link only               Direct NIP-07 posting   Bridge key + gh_user tag

User setup                    Nostr -> GitHub
----------------------------  -------------------------------------------------
No auth                       App installation + visible npub/event attribution
GitHub App user auth only     App installation; an unrelated browser Nostr
                              identity is not guessed
GitHub + Nostr linked         User's expiring GitHub App authorization when it
                              can write the repository; App fallback otherwise
Nostr link only               App installation + visible npub/event attribution

Thus auth.friendly-machines.com is optional for users in two independent
senses:

  - the standalone Nostr client signs directly with NIP-07;
  - the bridge crosses unlinked authors through its labelled bridge/App
    identities rather than rejecting their content.

Linking only upgrades attribution. It is never an intake gate.

Browser sessions are disposable views of durable identity state. Clearing a
cookie never removes or recreates a GitHub/Nostr link. The account page first
uses its HttpOnly session cookie; when that cookie is absent, "Sign in with
Nostr" uses a one-time, non-published NIP-07 signature to prove the existing
pubkey and issue a fresh cookie. That sign-in transition updates only
oauth_states. It never writes links or nostr_accounts. An unlinked signer is
reported as unlinked and cannot create an identity association through the
sign-in endpoint.

Connecting a remote NIP-46 signer is a separate operation. Repeating its proof
for an already-linked pubkey may restore a browser session, but preserves the
stored bunker and client key. Those credentials change only through the
explicit "Replace remote signer connection" action, and the replacement signer
must prove the exact existing pubkey.

After both identities are linked, the account page offers a separate optional
NIP-39 GitHub profile assertion. It is presentation metadata, not bridge
authority and not part of link completion. The user creates the one-file Gist
manually, so the GitHub App needs no Gists permission. A durable job verifies
the Gist owner and exact npub proof, reduces the current kind-10011 snapshot
and its deletions, preserves the complete existing tag/content set, and shows
the proposed set before any signature. Explicit confirmation is bound to the
observed head ID. The worker refetches immediately before signing; a different
head returns to review rather than overwriting unseen state.

The kind-10011 timestamp is monotonically greater than the observed head. Once
the linked NIP-46 signer returns the exact requested event, the complete signed
wire event is persisted before relay I/O. Stable per-event/per-relay jobs then
publish it independently, so a process crash or relay retry never requests
another signature. Kind-10002 NIP-65 write relays and bounded collaboration
relays are sets of publication/discovery alternatives; signer-transport relays
are never mistaken for user event relays. Removing the proof is another
reviewed complete replacement without the managed GitHub claim; it does not
unlink the bridge or delete the Gist.

One GitHub limitation is intrinsic to repository stars: installation tokens
are not user identities and GitHub's star/unstar endpoints accept GitHub App
user tokens, not installation tokens. GitHub -> Nostr stars retain the normal
bridge-key fallback for unlinked GitHub users. A Nostr -> GitHub star remains
durable until that Nostr author links a GitHub user with Starring permission;
the bridge never pretends that the App installation starred it.

3. FIXED SERVER CONFIGURATION CONTRACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The runtime uses exactly:

  /home/dh_4nnf3n/secrets

This is a literal deployment path because mod_fcgid does not provide HOME.
The runtime does not walk parent directories, inspect document-root values,
scan filenames, inspect token formats, use home-directory markers, or look
for renamed/older secret files.

Required files:

  github-webhook.php            returns the GitHub App webhook secret string
  github-app.php                returns:
                                  [
                                    "client_id" => "...",
                                    "client_secret" => "..."
                                  ]
  github-app-private-key.pem    the App's generated RSA private key
  repo-registration.php        repository-registration shared secret string
  db-crypt.php                  64 lowercase/uppercase hex characters
  bridge-key.php                64-hex Nostr bridge private key

The secrets directory must be owned by the server account with mode 0700.
Every secret file must be owned by that account, must not be a symlink, and
must have mode 0600.

There is one GitHub App. It supplies both:

  - installation tokens, minted automatically for always-on repository work;
  - optional expiring user tokens and refresh tokens for personal attribution.

There is no standalone OAuth App path and no static access-token file.

4. REPOSITORY AUTHORITY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

GitHub authority has two independent requirements:

  1. An active GitHub App installation contains the exact repository.
     Webhook payloads may bank an unknown installation/repository, but it is
     not usable until REST reconciliation has succeeded and minted a token.
     Delayed webhook payloads cannot reactivate a removed repository or
     deleted/suspended installation. There is no separate account-authority
     cache: the current installation and exact repository row are authority.
     Hourly complete App-installation discovery bootstraps or repairs state
     even if the installation webhook itself was missed.

  2. repos.php contains an operator-authenticated mapping from the GitHub
     full name to an exact signed NIP-34 30617 address and relay set.

Registration verifies the newest addressable 30617 across every configured
relay and banks the complete signed event in the durable repository-
announcement ledger. Newest means greatest created_at; the lowest event ID
wins an exact timestamp tie. The registered relay set must equal the complete
relay set in the winning event's relays tags.

Relay polling retains 30617 replacements and same-author kind-5 address
deletions as two independent input sets. Their reducer is arrival-order
independent: a deletion suppresses announcements at or before its created_at,
while a later replacement restores the address. Under NIP-09 every a tag is a
deletion target, never repository context. Content and star deletions therefore
emit only exact e targets and must not copy the repository a tag. An empty
EOSE, incomplete query, relay failure, or process restart never erases the
banked announcement.

The 30617 publisher is implicitly a maintainer unless the announcement has a
u tag. It does not list itself in maintainers. bridge_authorized records only
whether the separate bridge key may author a status for someone else's root;
ordinary issue/comment crossing does not depend on it. Relay unavailability
is not interpreted as bridge_authorized=false.

Every crossing reloads the reduced durable mapping and its banked 30617;
relay I/O is not part of the crossing transaction. Queued payload snapshots
are not authority after a replacement or deletion is reduced. There is no
name-derived repository fallback.

5. GITHUB -> NOSTR
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

github/webhook.php:

  1. accepts POST only and caps the body;
  2. verifies X-Hub-Signature-256 with github-webhook.php;
  3. ignores deliveries without a GitHub App installation, except the App
     user-authorization revocation event;
  4. durably enqueues the complete authenticated delivery;
  5. returns 202 without doing relay I/O.

Cron consumes the raw delivery. Unready repository/App state raises a
dependency deferral; it does not consume the failed-execution budget.

Issue opened:

  - creates a regular kind 1621 event with a, p, subject, gh, and gh-N tags;
  - uses the GitHub source created_at, never the queue time;
  - an active GitHub/Nostr link fixes the signer to that user's NIP-46 key;
  - temporary signer failure leaves the durable job pending and can never
    produce an immutable bridge-signed substitute;
  - no active link: bridge signature plus gh_user attribution.

Issue title/body edited:

  Kind 1621 is a regular immutable event. NIP-34 defines no interoperable
  issue-edit event. Replacing the root would create a different identity and
  disconnect existing comments/status references. The bridge therefore emits
  a kind 1111 NIP-22 correction notice on the original root containing the
  changed title/body. It does not pretend that the original event mutated.

Comment created:

  - creates kind 1111;
  - E/K/P identify the immutable issue root;
  - e/k/p identify the parent;
  - GitHub issue comments are flat, so a GitHub-created comment is top-level.

Comment edited:

  NIP-22 comments are regular immutable events. The edit becomes another
  attributed kind 1111 notice on the original root.

Repository star/unstar:

  The GitHub `star` webhook is only a wake-up signal. The worker queries the
  current starred-repository collection of the webhook's actor and matches by
  stable numeric repository/user IDs. Current starred state creates one
  NIP-25 kind-7 `⭐` reaction carrying e, a, p, and k=30617 references to the
  verified repository announcement. Current unstarred state publishes a
  same-author kind-5 deletion for every still active reaction belonging to
  that logical GitHub pair.

  GitHub restricted `/repos/{owner}/{repo}/stargazers` to repository admins
  and collaborators in July 2026. An App installation is a separate
  authorization relationship, not a collaborator role. Consequently, signed
  Star webhooks establish new stable actor/repository pairs in a
  primary-keyed SQLite set; hourly reduction combines those pairs with mapped
  reactions and rechecks each pair independently. A webhook GitHub never
  delivers for a previously unknown actor cannot be reconstructed from the
  restricted repository-wide API. Received webhooks are durably banked before
  HTTP 202, and no arrival ordering is assumed.

  The `watch` webhook is not used. It is a legacy/incomplete star-added signal
  and has no removal action.

Open/close:

  - reopened                         kind 1630 Open
  - closed, state_reason=completed   kind 1631 Resolved
  - other closed                     kind 1632 Closed

NIP-34 permits a status only from the root author or a repository maintainer.
The bridge key signs when it authored the root or is currently a maintainer.
Otherwise the linked root signer must sign; the bridge does not forge an
unauthorized status.

GitHub source deletion:

  If the target was originally mirrored from GitHub, the bridge publishes a
  same-signer kind 5 request, stores both the event crossing and deletion
  relationship, and can repair the relationship after a
  publish-before-ledger crash without republishing.

  If GitHub deletes its mirror of an independently authored Nostr root, the
  bridge does not delete the Nostr source. It records that the GitHub mirror is
  gone so later out-of-order dependants do not retry an impossible crossing.

6. NOSTR -> GITHUB
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Cron collects every in-scope, correctly signed event, regardless of whether
its author linked an identity. Authentication selects the GitHub author; it
does not select which events may cross.

Issue:

  A signed kind 1621 whose a/p tags match a verified repository creates a
  GitHub issue. An unlinked author is visibly attributed by npub and Nostr
  event ID in the body and the App installation performs the write.

Comment:

  The E/K/P root and e/k/p parent references must be complete and must match
  the actual stored authors and kinds. A nested parent must belong to the same
  root. The GitHub comment is posted to the issue after the complete chain is
  available. GitHub displays it flat; event_map retains the Nostr parent ID.

Status:

  The author must be the root author, the repository publisher (when implicit
  maintainer), or a maintainer in the current winning 30617. The status must
  also carry p tags for both the repository publisher and root author.

  - 1630 -> GitHub open/reopened
  - 1631 -> GitHub closed/completed
  - 1632 -> GitHub closed/not_planned
  - 1633 -> stored as the valid Nostr status head; no fake GitHub state

The valid Nostr head is greatest created_at, then lowest event ID. An older
event arriving later is stored but cannot overwrite newer GitHub state.

Repository star:

  A signed kind-7 reaction whose content is exactly `⭐` and whose unambiguous
  e/a/p/k tags identify a verified kind-30617 repository is a star. The
  corresponding linked GitHub App user token calls GitHub's authenticated-user
  star endpoint. A same-author kind-5 e-target removes it. If the author has
  not linked GitHub, the star and any later deletion remain durable pending
  work; an installation token cannot impersonate a GitHub user.

  The client publishes a distinct reaction rather than rewriting the user's
  NIP-51 kind-10018 global followed-repositories list. That avoids losing
  unrelated list entries during concurrent client updates.

Deletion:

  A kind 5 e-target is effective only when its author equals the actual target
  author. Deleting a deletion request has no effect.

  - mapped GitHub comments are deleted through the REST comment DELETE API;
  - GitHub has no general REST issue deletion operation, so an issue becomes a
    clearly labelled, closed tombstone;
  - deleting the current status reveals the next valid status (or default
    Open) and applies it when GitHub can represent it;
  - a Nostr root deletion is permanent locally; later GitHub edits/reopens are
    restored to the tombstone.

7. DISTRIBUTED ORDERING AND CONVERGENCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Neither GitHub webhook delivery nor relay delivery is causal order.
created_at orders replacement/status winners and siblings; it does not prove
parent-before-child arrival.

The bridge therefore:

  - stores the authenticated raw GitHub delivery before acknowledging it;
  - reconstructs/queues a GitHub root from comment, edit, status, or deletion
    payloads when Opened was delayed or absent;
  - records a discovered Nostr root synchronously before dependent execution;
  - queues comments/statuses/deletions by immutable target ID even when their
    dependency is absent;
  - defers unresolved dependencies without increasing fails;
  - runs root-specific historical scans for E-rooted comments, e-rooted
    statuses, and e-targeted deletions;
  - keeps separate resumable relay cursors and performs hourly full sweeps so
    an event published late with an old created_at is still found;
  - verifies every relay result against the exact local filter before it can
    move a cursor;
  - merges relay results by event ID and reduces statuses/replacements
    deterministically;
  - banks verified 30617 heads and their k=30617 address deletions as
    independent durable sets; an empty EOSE cannot unverify a repository;
  - treats deletion requests as durable tombstones and reapplies them after a
    late target appears;
  - treats each GitHub star delivery as a reconciliation trigger, queries
    current GitHub state, and assigns monotonically increasing logical Nostr
    seconds when separate star generations share one GitHub timestamp;
  - retains pending unlinked Nostr star IDs in deletion polling, so an unstar
    can arrive before the star crosses or long before the author links GitHub.

Nostr filters have no standard offset or event-ID tie cursor. If a relay caps
one exact filter at more events sharing one created_at second than its limit,
no generic client can prove it retrieved the omitted same-timestamp events.
Root-specific backfills, separate filters, a 5000-event browser limit, hourly
server sweeps, and repeated relay queries reduce this risk but cannot change
that protocol/relay limitation.

8. IDEMPOTENCE AND CRASH WINDOWS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

event_map is unique in both GitHub ID and Nostr ID directions.

GitHub -> Nostr:

  The unsigned event is deterministic from the durable job. Before signing or
  publishing, the worker asks the configured relays for the exact expected
  event ID. If publication succeeded before SQLite committed, the worker
  verifies the full recovered event and records it without republishing. A
  linked author has exactly one deterministic signer identity: the user's key.
  Relay recovery therefore checks only that user-signed form.

Nostr -> GitHub:

  Created issues/comments carry an HMAC-authenticated hidden marker derived
  from db-crypt.php and the Nostr event ID. The durable queue timestamp bounds
  paginated reconciliation and excludes objects created before the job.
  Recovery accepts one unique eligible object from the complete bounded result
  set; it never selects an event-map identity by mutable updated_at, integer
  timestamp, or numeric ID. A user cannot forge a marker before the bridge
  publishes it. If a now-public valid marker appears on multiple eligible
  objects, the job defers for explicit resolution rather than guessing which
  object is the crossing.

Only the original durable crossing job may create its ledger row. A signed
proxy tag is a loop-kill/origin claim, not permission to invent a mapping.

Transient GitHub/relay failures remain pending with capped backoff. Immutable
invalid inputs complete as explicit permanent skips. Repeated logic/config
failures become inspectable dead jobs after eight failed executions.
Automatic redelivery/full scans do not create a fresh failure budget for the
same immutable input; operator repair is explicit.

9. DATABASE CONTRACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SQLite uses WAL, foreign keys, busy_timeout, an adjacent cron flock, and mode
0600 for the database, WAL/SHM, lock, and logs.

An empty database initializes directly to schema version 6 and its exact
schema-contract marker. A non-empty database must already satisfy that
contract and the required table/column/foreign-key shape. Runtime performs no
incremental, additive, or compatibility migration and never searches for old
credentials.

Important roles:

  github_installations / github_installation_repositories
      authoritative App access and encrypted cached installation token

  github_accounts / nostr_accounts / links
      optional one-to-one identities and encrypted user authorization/bunker

  repos
      immutable one-to-one mapping between an exact GitHub full name and an
      exact verified (publisher pubkey, 30617 d identifier) address

  jobs
      durable raw deliveries, crossings, dependency work, backfills, optional
      NIP-39 review/sign state, and per-relay publication retries

  event_map
      completed bidirectional crossings and immutable parent/root metadata

  nostr_roots
      roots known before, during, or after their GitHub crossing

  deletion_map
      durable, authorized deletion event -> target relationships

  poll_cursor
      resumable per-relay, per-filter scans

10. SECURITY BOUNDARIES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - webhook input is HMAC-authenticated before logging/queuing;
  - every Nostr event ID/signature and exact subscription match is verified;
  - p/P/t membership checks use only tag element 1; a relay-hint or extension
    slot cannot impersonate an expected pubkey or label;
  - scalar fields and root/parent relations reject conflicting values rather
    than selecting by tag order;
  - OAuth state is single-use, server-side, cookie-bound, and uses PKCE;
  - linking status is available only to a browser holding its unguessable
    HttpOnly session cookie or completing a signed, single-use NIP-07 recovery
    challenge; a claimed pubkey alone cannot query GitHub links;
  - NIP-07 sign-in consumes an empty oauth_states row through a five-minute
    signed challenge,
    exposes only a server-keyed HMAC rather than the HttpOnly cookie value,
    and never creates a link or replaces NIP-46 credentials;
  - ordinary repeated NIP-46 proof preserves existing signer credentials;
    only an explicit cookie-bound replacement request may rotate them, and
    only after the new signer proves the exact existing pubkey;
  - optional NIP-39 status and mutations require the exact live, fully linked
    cookie-bound GitHub/Nostr pair; GET is read-only, Gist URLs are fixed to
    gist.github.com and the linked login, and no Gists permission is granted;
  - NIP-39 confirmation names the exact reduced head reviewed by the user; a
    concurrent replacement forces review again, and the signed event is
    durably recorded before independent relay publication;
  - plain Nostr-link page GET is read-only; an explicit start POST allocates
    the browser session and per-link keypair, while approval listener/intake
    mutations are POST-only;
  - GitHub authorization initiation is POST-only; the externally mandated
    OAuth callback remains a single-use, PKCE-protected GET bound to that
    browser session;
  - signer authorization challenges expose only validated HTTPS URLs to that
    same cookie-bound browser, while the original NIP-46 request ID remains
    subscribed for the eventual response;
  - stable GitHub numeric user IDs prevent login rename/reuse from moving a
    Nostr link to another account;
  - user and installation tokens, bunker URIs, and per-link NIP-46 client keys
    are encrypted at rest;
  - NIP-46 completion stores the signer's current relay set as required by
    switch_relays, after a four-endpoint cap, strict wss URL validation, and
    public-address-only, single-resolution WebSocket connection checks that
    prevent private-network and DNS-rebinding SSRF;
  - cron keeps all advertised NIP-46 response subscriptions open past EOSE
    during each scan, because kind 24133 approval responses are ephemeral;
  - get_public_key is accepted only after the signer returns a valid signature
    under that claimed user key for an exact, non-published link challenge;
  - the bridge never receives a user's Nostr private key;
  - GitHub user-token failure falls back to the App instead of dropping work;
  - NIP-46 signer failure falls back only for ordinary issue/comment content,
    never where NIP-34/NIP-09 requires the original/authorized signer;
  - database/log/include/test paths are denied by .htaccess;
  - cron.php is CLI-only;
  - user content is escaped in the browser client and server HTML;
  - public HTTP failures do not expose secret filesystem paths.

11. CLIENT CONTRACT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

client/index.html is deployable at any directory containing projects.list and
the listed Git repositories. Repository URLs are relative to that deployed
file; it has no dependency on /git/, /auth/, or auth.friendly-machines.com.

Its Nostr event reducer, comment tree, patch graph, PR/patch relationship,
replacement, status, deletion, and route behavior are documented in
client/README.md.

The client currently loads pinned, SRI-checked browser Git/Nostr libraries
from CDNs. This is a deployment dependency, not a GitHub dependency and not
server bridge authority. Self-hosting those exact assets would remove the CDN
availability dependency, but no asset is downloaded or executed by the server.

